<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Mazyadid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">961 CE – 1150 CE (approx.) · capital Hilla</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Arab Shia dynasty of the Banu Asad that ruled central Iraq along the Euphrates and founded the city of Hilla.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Mazyadids, leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-asad">Banu Asad</a>, held lands along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Their founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-mazyad">Ali ibn Mazyad</a>, was recognised by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a> in the later 10th century. The family controlled the farming districts and the routes to the Shia shrine cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Mazyadids were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shia</a> and patrons of Shia scholarship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadaqa-ibn-mansur">Sadaqa ibn Mansur</a>, the greatest of the line, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilla">al-Hilla</a> around 1101 on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> near ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, which became a major centre of Shia learning. The dynasty balanced between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>; Sadaqa sheltered rebels and was killed fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-tapar">Muhammad Tapar</a> in 1108. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubays-ibn-sadaqa">Dubays</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Zengi</a> and other enemies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, the Mazyadids lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilla">Hilla</a>, and by the mid-12th century the revived Abbasid caliphate had ended their rule. Hilla remained an important Shia city.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mazyadid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Mazyadid dynasty" width="320" height="200"><figcaption>Image: MWahaiibii · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banu_Mazyad_Emirate_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>961 CE – 1150 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hilla</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali ibn Mazyad, Sadaqa ibn Mansur, Dubays ibn Sadaqa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Twelver Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mazyadids, Banu Mazyad</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1056&amp;focus=mazyadid-dynasty" aria-label="Mazyadid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5776 993 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mazyadid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6367 1352h0M6481 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6096 1249h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6096 1249h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iraq. <a href="/atlas?year=1056&amp;focus=mazyadid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

